<section class="page-head"><div class="container"><span class="status-pill completed">Completed</span><div class="eyebrow">Applied ML · Ranking · Leakage Control</div><h1>Term Deposit Customer Ranking</h1><p class="section-lead">Can a bank get more term-deposit subscriptions from the same calling capacity by ranking already eligible customers before the call?</p></div></section>
<section><div class="container content">
<section class="story-section"><h2>Project at a glance</h2><div class="story-grid"><div class="story-step"><strong>1 · Objective</strong><p>Prioritise already-eligible customers before a sales call so limited calling capacity is focused on the customers most likely to subscribe.</p></div><div class="story-step"><strong>2 · Data</strong><p>40,000 historical marketing calls, including 2,896 subscriptions: a 7.24% conversion rate.</p></div><div class="story-step"><strong>3 · Method</strong><p>Compare Logistic Regression with HistGradientBoosting using only information available before the call; post-call duration is used only as a leakage diagnostic.</p></div><div class="story-step"><strong>4 · Evaluation</strong><p>Use an untouched holdout, expanding-window ordered validation and lift at practical call depths rather than relying on a single classification metric.</p></div><div class="story-step story-impact"><strong>5 · Outcome</strong><p>Logistic Regression was the more robust pilot candidate and achieved 1.853857× lift at 5% call depth and 1.401474× at 20% in retrospective evaluation.</p></div></div></section>
<h2>Context and objective</h2>
<p>The output is not primarily a yes/no classifier. It is a pre-call ranking score used to order eligible customers so limited calling capacity is spent on the most promising records first.</p>

<h2>Data and constraints</h2>
<ul>
<li>40,000 historical calls.</li>
<li>2,896 subscriptions: a 7.24% conversion rate.</li>
<li>13 original predictor fields; 12 are valid before the call.</li>
<li><code>duration</code> is only known after the call finishes, so it is retained solely as a leakage diagnostic and excluded from every deployable model.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Technical solution</h2>
<p>Logistic Regression provides the transparent linear candidate and HistGradientBoosting the nonlinear challenger. Model selection uses PR-AUC as the primary objective, while deployment judgement also considers ordered robustness, lift, interpretability and monitoring simplicity.</p>

<h2>Evaluation and evidence</h2>
<ul>
<li>Untouched 20% holdout: tuned HGB PR-AUC / ROC-AUC = <strong>0.272820 / 0.733930</strong>; tuned LR = <strong>0.240265 / 0.709681</strong>.</li>
<li>Expanding-window ordered validation: LR = <strong>0.112626 / 0.566290</strong>; HGB = <strong>0.096333 / 0.551600</strong>.</li>
<li>The large shuffled-to-ordered performance drop is treated as a central robustness warning, not hidden.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout">Prediction timing is part of the model definition. The post-call <code>duration</code> feature produces a large retrospective performance jump, which is exactly why it is evidence of leakage rather than a deployable predictor.</div>

<h2>Key results</h2>
<p>Across eight eligible forward blocks, Logistic Regression captured <strong>228 subscribers in the top-ranked 5%</strong> of calls versus about <strong>123 expected under random calling</strong> at the same volume.</p>
<ul>
<li>5% call depth: <strong>1.853857×</strong> lift.</li>
<li>10% call depth: <strong>1.606389×</strong> lift.</li>
<li>20% call depth: <strong>1.401474×</strong> lift.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Limitations and scope</h2>
<p>The evidence is retrospective and the data do not provide a verified customer identifier, campaign identifier or trustworthy timestamp. Raw scores should be treated as ranking signals rather than literal subscription probabilities until prospective recalibration and new-campaign evidence are available.</p>

<h2>Practical relevance</h2>
<p>The recommended next step is a controlled prospective pilot using <strong>unweighted Logistic Regression with all 12 valid pre-call features</strong> as the primary candidate, with HGB retained as a challenger. The intended benefit is more subscriptions from the same calling capacity, not automated customer eligibility or guaranteed conversion.</p>

<h2>What this demonstrates</h2>
<p>Leakage control, decision-time feature design, comparison of transparent and nonlinear models, ordered validation, ranking evaluation and translation of model scores into a capacity-aware operating recommendation.</p>

